{
  "summary": "The third proposal's approach is largely sound but has three weaknesses worth addressing. First, the 'clear after consumption' strategy for human_rejection_feedback is fragile — if the implementing agent crashes or is killed before the feedback is consumed and cleared, the feedback is lost permanently since it was already removed from state; a safer approach is to leave the feedback in place and let it be naturally superseded when the item progresses past implementing (or simply check timestamp/stage transitions to determine relevance). Second, all three proposals overlook that the web UI currently sends target_stage='rejected' which routes to the terminal 'rejected' stage via _on_move_requested (tui.py:2168-2169) — the server-side handler has no special-casing for final-human-approval rejections, so even adding a reason field to the API won't help unless _on_move_requested is also taught to route final-human-approval items to 'implementing' instead of 'rejected'. The handler needs to check the item's current stage and override the target when appropriate. Third, the proposal underestimates the web UI change — it's not just adding a text prompt modal, but also changing the approveFeature() function to send a different target_stage (e.g., 'implementing' instead of 'rejected') along with the reason, which means the reject button's onclick handler (index.html:577) needs to be decoupled from the approve flow entirely rather than just adding a field to the existing request."
}
